# the argument of hume-treatise-3.md as claude-opus-5-5 read it: check that it says what the text says
# S1: the contrariety of passions would be of small danger but for a peculiarity in our outward circumstances.
# "May be ravished" and "may be transferred" are kept as plain atoms, not modal operators.
atom	danger	this contrariety of passions is attended with more than small danger
atom	peculiarity	the contrariety concurs with a peculiarity in our outward circumstances which affords it an opportunity of exerting itself
atom	secure_mind	we are perfectly secure in the enjoyment of the internal satisfaction of our minds
atom	ravish_body	the external advantages of our body may be ravished from us
atom	advantage_body	the external advantages of our body can be of advantage to him who deprives us of them
atom	exposed_poss	the possessions acquired by industry and good fortune are exposed to the violence of others
atom	transfer_poss	the possessions acquired by industry and good fortune may be transferred without suffering any loss or alteration
atom	scarce_poss	there is not a sufficient quantity of such possessions to supply every one's desires and necessities
atom	improvement_advantage	the improvement of these goods is the chief advantage of society
atom	impediment	the instability of the possession of these goods, along with their scarcity, is the chief impediment of society
P1	S1	¬peculiarity → ¬danger
P2	S3	secure_mind	kind empirical
P3	S4	ravish_body ∧ ¬advantage_body	kind empirical
P4	S5	exposed_poss ∧ transfer_poss ∧ scarce_poss	kind empirical
P5	S6	improvement_advantage
A1	-	exposed_poss ∧ transfer_poss ∧ scarce_poss → impediment
C	S6	impediment	from P4 A1
